[# Section 18 — the CX store platform: content addressing, the embedded
   workflow, git-porcelain and replication, the service tier, the XSP
   store profile + gRPC-edge transports, security/encryption, the
   management console. Spec home:
   the store spec (spec/03-approved/platform/store.md). The operator
   depth that used to sit in a separate developer set (docs/dev/store-*.md,
   folded here under DOCS-46) is on this page and in [[operations]]: every
   claim stands beside the store corpus case this guide's cx replayed when
   the page was built ([fixture id=]) or names the spec page that holds it,
   and every command and output was run against the branch-head binary
   before it landed here (issue #425 coverage gap: store platform user
   guide). #]

[section id=store group='The platform group' n=18 title='The store platform'

  [intro
    """The CX Store is the platform's persistence spine: a
       **content-addressed object store** behind one document API. The
       same code runs against an in-memory store, a file on disk, an
       SQLite file, an S3 bucket, or a production daemon — you pick the
       substrate with a URL at open time and nothing else changes. It
       is specified in the store spec
       (`spec/03-approved/platform/store.md`); this section teaches the
       workflow: the embedded tier, the porcelain and replication, the
       service tier and its wire, security, and the console. The
       per-function API reference is the `store` module page in the
       Standard library section of this guide; running the daemon —
       bootstrap, authority, observability, lifecycle — is
       [[operations]]."""]

  [child id=store-model n=18.1 title='Content addressing and identity'
    [body
      """A document's ID **is** its content: the SHA-256 of its strict
         canonical bytes (see [[hash]]). That single decision buys the
         rest of the platform: identity is invariant across substrates,
         encodings, host bindings, and the wire, so replication is
         verifiable by construction and identical content stores
         exactly once — dedup is free, not a feature:"""]
    [example lang=cx
      """[?lib 'cx-platform/store' :as store]
[?let [= $s [$store:open 'mem://']]
      [= $h [$store:put-doc $s [order [id 1] [amt 100]]]]
      [= $again [$store:put-doc $s [order [id 1] [amt 100]]]]
      [= $doc [$store:get-doc $s $h]]
  ([= $h $again], $doc)]"""]
    [output
      """(true, [order [id 1] [amt 100]])"""]
    [body
      """`get-doc` re-hashes after decode, so a corrupted object can
         never come back as a silently wrong document — an integrity
         mismatch is the hard error `CXER1120`. Documents split by
         **identity rule**: a *structured* document (cx data) is keyed
         by the hash of its canonical bytes, and the canonicalized
         round-trip above is the contract; an *opaque* document — CX
         code, images, plain text, anything whose bytes ARE the
         content — is keyed by the hash of its **raw bytes** via
         `put-blob`, and `get-blob` returns those bytes exactly. Code's
         "same function?" question is answered separately by the pure
         `[$cx:computation-id]` claim — an index, never a storage
         key — so alpha-equivalent definitions share one claim while
         each spelling keeps its own byte-exact address:"""]
    [example lang=cx
      """[?lib 'cx-platform/store' :as store]
[?let [= $s [$store:open 'mem://']]
      [= $src '[?def double ($x) [* $x 2]]']
      [= $back [$store:get-blob $s [$store:put-blob $s $src]]]
  ([= $back $src],
   [= [$cx:computation-id '[?def double ($x) [* $x 2]]']
      [$cx:computation-id '[?def double ($n) [* $n 2]]']])]"""]
    [output
      """(true, true)"""]
    [body
      """The substrate is chosen by the URL scheme at open time, and
         every effectful substrate is capability-gated deny-by-default
         (see [[capabilities]]):"""]
    [table
      [tr [th 'URL'] [th 'substrate'] [th 'grant needed']]
      [tr [td 'mem://'] [td 'in-process memory'] [td 'none — capability-free']]
      [tr [td 'file:///path'] [td 'local directory (pack format)'] [td '--allow-read / --allow-write']]
      [tr [td 'sqlite:///path.db'] [td 'single SQLite file'] [td '--allow-read / --allow-write']]
      [tr [td 's3://bucket/prefix'] [td 'S3-compatible object storage'] [td '--allow-net']]
      [tr [td 'http(s):// · ftp:// · sftp://'] [td 'remote backends'] [td '--allow-net']]
      [tr [td 'cx-store://name/ · cx-store+xsp://host:port/name/'] [td 'the service daemon over THE store wire (next subsections)'] [td '--allow-net=host:port']]]
    [note
      """By default a store decomposes each document into a Merkle
         graph of shared subtree objects — identical subtrees across
         documents store once, a one-field edit re-stores only the path
         to the root, and `diff` skips identical subtrees in O(1). The
         alternative `document+` model (one object per doc, the
         required prefix for the columnar encodings) is the other
         axis of the same URL —
         `[document+]<substrate>://<location>[?encoding=…&read-only=…&schema=…]`
         — and we ask you to state only what departs from a default,
         only at create: a `file://` store is self-describing on
         reopen. An axis we have specified but not applied (a
         non-`none` `?compression=`, the `?cache=` layer) is refused
         fail-closed, never accepted and ignored, and a scheme we do
         not build dispatches to a refusal that names it:"""]
    [fixture id=store-025b-open-unknown-scheme-dispatch]]

  [child id=store-embedded n=18.2 title='The embedded workflow'
    [body
      """`cx-platform/store` is the embedded tier — the store runs inside
         your process. Documents are immutable; "the latest X" lives in
         a separate **alias layer** that maps names to hashes, which is
         the one mutable surface. Updating means storing a new document
         (or deriving one with `modify-doc`, which applies any
         `[?modify]` action server-side-shaped and returns the new
         hash) and re-pointing the name:"""]
    [example lang=cx
      """[?lib 'cx-platform/store' :as store]
[?let [= $s [$store:open 'mem://']]
      [= $v1 [$store:put-doc $s [config [retries 3]]]]
      [= $v2 [$store:modify-doc $s $v1 [set-attr name=env value='prod']]]
      [= $b [$store:branch $s 'main' $v2]]
  ([$store:get-doc $s [$store:get-alias $s 'main']],
   [$store:get-doc $s $v1],
   [$store:diff $s $v1 $v2])]"""]
    [output
      """([config env=prod [retries 3]], [config [retries 3]], [diff [change path='/config' kind=modified]])"""]
    [body
      """The original is untouched — history is free because nothing is
         ever overwritten. Across the whole corpus, `query` runs a
         CXPath selection over every held document:"""]
    [example lang=cx
      """[?lib 'cx-platform/store' :as store]
[?let [= $s [$store:open 'mem://']]
      [= $h1 [$store:put-doc $s [invoice [total 120] [customer 'ada']]]]
      [= $h2 [$store:put-doc $s [invoice [total 80] [customer 'grace']]]]
  [$store:query $s '//total']]"""]
    [output
      """([result doc=sha2-256:e5b689fa494377cea041d4bf6496e28e97dd13a059aa4c22c75360fc7d0ceb30 source='mem://#//total' [total 120]], [result doc='sha2-256:89123d4330eda07c2dbeb3878cd1ca12760c1d19e37fdb634b4737bfc2784924' source='mem://#//total' [total 80]])"""]
    [body
      """Going durable is changing the URL — the same program against
         `file://` persists, and a reopen finds the store
         self-describing (options are declared at create, not repeated
         at open):"""]
    [example lang=cx
      """[?lib 'cx-platform/store' :as store]
[?let [= $s [$store:open 'file:///tmp/notes-store']]
  [$store:put-doc $s [note [text 'durable']]]]"""]
    [example lang=sh
      """$ cx --allow-read --allow-write notes.cx"""]
    [body
      """Three habits worth forming from day one: open read paths
         read-only (`[$store:open-opts URL [map read-only='true']]` —
         then only the `read` grant is ever requested, and a write
         raises `CXER1110`); treat a Store handle as **single-owner**
         (sharing one across `[par]` workers raises `CXER1140` — open a
         handle per worker); and let denials teach you the grant — an
         ungranted open is a `CXER0271` error value naming the exact
         flag, so a program can degrade gracefully (see
         [[caps-errors]]). Streaming variants (`put-doc-stream`,
         `iter-docs`) keep memory bounded over any corpus size; the
         full surface is on the `store` module page. The read-only
         refusal and the ungranted open, replayed:"""]
    [fixture id=store-ro-001-readonly-put]
    [fixture id=store-001-open-cap-denied]
    [body
      """**One writer per local root.** A `file://`, `sqlite://` or
         local columnar root admits ONE writable handle plus any number
         of read-only ones — across processes as much as within one. A
         second writable open in the same process returns a handle over
         the same live store; a second writable open from another
         process is refused `CXER1143`, naming the holding pid. Two
         writers on one root corrupt it, and the damage only shows at a
         later open — so when you need many writers, run the service
         tier (one writer, N clients) or open read-only. A crashed
         writer releases the root at once: the lock dies with the
         process, and nothing is cleaned up by hand (the store spec's
         locking section). Streaming and direct storage agree byte for
         byte, and an opaque blob comes back exactly as it went in:"""]
    [fixture id=store-017-put-doc-stream-equals-put-doc]
    [fixture id=store-blob-001-byte-exact-round-trip]
    [body """The errors you meet first, each a value naming its cause
            (the store spec's error-codes section carries the table):"""]
    [table
      [tr [th 'code'] [th 'meaning']]
      [tr [td 'CXER1100 / CXER1101'] [td 'unknown or unbuilt backend / unreachable substrate']]
      [tr [td 'CXER1110'] [td 'a write on a read-only handle']]
      [tr [td 'CXER1116'] [td 'a substrate persist failed — never a phantom success; the next successful persist self-heals']]
      [tr [td 'CXER1120 / CXER1121'] [td 'integrity mismatch on read (never a silent wrong document) / not found']]
      [tr [td 'CXER1130 / CXER1140 / CXER1143'] [td 'closed handle / a handle shared across workers / a second writable open of one root from another process']]]]

  [child id=store-porcelain n=18.3 title='Porcelain — introspect, branch, replicate'
    [body
      """Over the content-addressed plumbing sits a deliberately
         git-shaped porcelain. Introspection and space reclamation:"""]
    [example lang=cx
      """[?lib 'cx-platform/store' :as store]
[?let [= $s [$store:open 'mem://']]
      [= $h1 [$store:put-doc $s [doc [v 1]]]]
      [= $h2 [$store:put-doc $s [doc [v 2]]]]
      [= $del [$store:delete-doc $s $h1]]
      [= $st [$store:status $s]]
      [= $gcr [$store:gc $s]]
  ($st@docs, $gcr@reclaimed, [$count [$store:log $s]])]"""]
    [output
      """(1, 4, 3)"""]
    [table
      [tr [th 'verb'] [th 'what it does']]
      [tr [td 'status'] [td 'held docs, distinct objects, dedup ratio, unflushed refs']]
      [tr [td 'log'] [td 'the linear ref-log in per-ref advance order — every insert, retract, and ref/alias advance is one recorded epoch (a delete is an act in the history, not a disappearance)']]
      [tr [td 'prune / gc'] [td 'reclaim objects no live ref reaches; gc adds durable compaction. A shared subtree survives the deletion of another doc — an object stays live while ANY ref reaches it']]
      [tr [td 'branch / branch-force'] [td 'point a mutable named ref at a doc; a non-fast-forward move is refused (CXER1114, CAS-safe) unless forced']]
      [tr [td 'diff'] [td 'structural diff by hash — identical subtrees are skipped, so cost is O(changed), not O(size)']]]
    [body """Each verb, replayed: a delete is an epoch in the log, a
            branch advance appears in it, `gc` reclaims only what no
            ref reaches, a non-fast-forward branch move is refused, and
            a one-leaf change diffs to one path:"""]
    [fixture id=store-log-003-branch-advances-appear]
    [fixture id=store-gc-001-reclaims-unreachable]
    [fixture id=store-branch-003-cas-conflict]
    [fixture id=store-diff-001-modified-leaf]
    [body
      """Replication is the same idea pointed at two stores. Because
         identity is content, transfer is incremental (only missing
         objects move), idempotent, and conflict-free — no CAS is
         needed on doc keys:"""]
    [example lang=cx
      """[?lib 'cx-platform/store' :as store]
[?let [= $laptop [$store:open 'mem://']]
      [= $h [$store:put-doc $laptop [doc [item 'hello']]]]
      [= $backup [$store:open 'mem://']]
      [= $r [$store:push $laptop $backup]]
  [$store:get-doc $backup $h]]"""]
    [output
      """[doc [item 'hello']]"""]
    [body
      """`push`/`pull`/`fetch` are the git-shaped incremental verbs;
         `clone` is an object-identity copy into an empty destination;
         `migrate` copies every doc and alias losslessly across **any**
         axis change (substrate, model, encoding) with every
         content-hash ID preserved. Backup is a `clone` to a second
         substrate; restore is a `clone` back, with every hash
         re-verifying on read; a substrate move (dev `mem://` to prod
         `s3://`, embedded to daemon) is a `migrate`. `merge` and
         `rebase` deliberately do not exist: the model keeps roots plus
         epoch-ordered refs, not a parent-linked commit graph. The
         three verbs, replayed — a clone into an empty destination, a
         push that carries only what the other side lacks, and a
         migration's report:"""]
    [fixture id=store-clone-001-into-empty]
    [fixture id=store-push-001-local-to-remote]
    [fixture id=store-mig-001-report]
    [body """The recovery playbook we run ourselves, in four lines:"""]
    [list
      [item """**Backup** is a `clone` (or a `push`) to a second
              substrate — nightly `file://` to `s3://` — incremental and
              verifiable by construction, because identity is
              content."""]
      [item """**Restore** is a `clone` back into a fresh store; every
              hash re-verifies on read."""]
      [item """**A substrate move** (file to sqlite, dev `mem://` to prod
              `s3://`, embedded to daemon) is a `migrate`; document
              identity is universal, so nothing that references a hash
              changes."""]
      [item """**Crash safety inside one store** is the engine's
              append-only ref-log recovery plus a per-op atomic-rename
              flush; a failed persist raises `CXER1116` rather than
              acknowledging a phantom write, and the next successful
              persist self-heals."""]]
    [body
      """Object-identity transfer is `subtree` to `subtree` only; across
         the model boundary the verbs degrade to the doc-level
         `migrate`. On a service handle (`cx-store://`, next subsection)
         `status` and `gc` are the daemon's admin-plane ops under the
         same names, scoped to the mount the session attached to;
         `mounts` enumerates the daemon's stores and `config-reload`
         triggers the validate-then-swap reload. All four sit in the
         `admin` class of the one grant table, and all four are
         daemon-level — a local handle IS its only store, so there they
         refuse with `CXER1709`:"""]
    [fixture id=store-mounts-001-local-handle-refuses]
    [fixture id=store-config-reload-001-local-handle-refuses]]

  [child id=store-service n=18.4 title='The service tier — cx store-serve'
    [body
      """When the store must outlive one process or serve many, the
         **same** API, wire format, and content addressing move behind
         a purpose-built daemon: `cx store-serve` (also covered from
         the operator side in [[ops-store-daemon]]). It adds the
         operational layer — XSP-AUTH grant-table authority, tenancy,
         observability, DoS fairness — and no storage smarts. The config is a CX document;
         one daemon mounts many named stores, each mount any embedded
         substrate URL:"""]
    [example lang=cx
      """[cxstore-service
  [bind addr="127.0.0.1:18971"]
  [stores [store name="docs" url="mem://docs"]]]"""]
    [example lang=sh
      """$ cx store-serve --config svc.cx --allow-net=127.0.0.1:18971"""]
    [output
      """cx store-serve: listening on tcp://127.0.0.1:18971 — 1 store(s): docs (mem://docs), 4 workers, auth OPEN — no [xsp [grants …]] configured, anonymous full access (configure grants for production)"""]
    [body
      """Config validation is **attr-exact**: an unknown attribute is a
         startup error (`CXER1711`), never a silent drop. Liveness and
         readiness are plain HTTP, and the binary doubles as its own
         probe — `cx store-health` exits 0 iff the daemon reports
         accepting, which is what the Docker HEALTHCHECK and load
         balancers call:"""]
    [example lang=sh
      """$ curl -s http://127.0.0.1:18971/cx-store/v1/health
[health [status "ok"]]
$ curl -s http://127.0.0.1:18971/cx-store/v1/ready
[ready [accepting true] [draining false]]
$ cx store-health --url http://127.0.0.1:18971/cx-store/v1/ready && echo ready
ready"""]
    [body
      """A client program is unchanged except for the URL — a
         `cx-store://` handle (the XSP store profile — THE store wire;
         `cx-store+xsp://` is the cleartext-dev sibling) is a named
         remote store with the identical API:"""]
    [example lang=cx
      """[?lib 'cx-platform/store' :as store]
[?let [= $remote [$store:open 'cx-store+xsp://127.0.0.1:18971/docs/']]
      [= $h [$store:put-doc $remote [doc [title 'over the wire']]]]
  [$store:get-doc $remote $h]]"""]
    [output
      """$ cx --allow-net=127.0.0.1:18971 wire.cx
[doc [title 'over the wire']]"""]
    [body
      """Authority is XSP-AUTH, deny-by-default: a client presents a
         verifiable credential (a DID it holds, plus an optional
         delegation chain), and the daemon's `[xsp [grants …]]` table —
         principal-rooted delegations over the capability classes
         read/write/delete/admin — decides per op. There is ONE
         authority calculus; the retired bearer/RBAC plane and the
         `[auth …]` config section are gone. A client names its
         identity through open-opts (`xsp-did` + `xsp-seed-env`); the
         seed never rides a URL."""]
    [body
      """Lifecycle: SIGTERM drains bounded and checkpoints every
         mounted store; SIGHUP hot-reloads the reloadable config
         sections (tls, timeouts, limits, observability) with
         validate-then-swap, all-or-nothing (`CXER1712` names any
         restart-required offender and refuses the whole reload).
         Two rules the URL enforces rather than documents: the port is
         required on `cx-store(+xsp)://` — the profile listener has no
         registered default, so a portless URL refuses at open rather
         than dialing something arbitrary — and the URL carries no
         userinfo (a `user@host` authority refuses with `CXER1100`;
         identity rides the open-opts, the seed always from the
         environment). The retired `cx-store+http(s)://` schemes refuse
         with `CXER1100` naming the replacements — a cutover, no
         dual-accept window. And like every remote substrate, a
         service open is capability-gated:"""]
    [fixture id=store-022-open-service-cap-denied]
    [body
      """Bootstrapping a deployment from a clean state (mint, grant,
         serve, present), the one authority calculus, metrics and
         tracing, DoS fairness, the reload's hot and restart-required
         sections, and the systemd/Docker artifacts under
         `tooling/cxstore/` are the operator's page: [[ops-bootstrap]],
         [[ops-authority]], [[ops-observability]], [[ops-artifacts]]."""]]

  [child id=store-transports n=18.5 title='Transports — the store wire and the edge'
    [body
      """The daemon serves the same store surface over two wires —
         same ops, same semantics, same error codes:"""]
    [table
      [tr [th 'transport'] [th 'client URL scheme'] [th 'role']]
      [tr [td 'XSP store profile'] [td 'cx-store:// (TLS) · cx-store+xsp:// (cleartext dev)'] [td 'THE CX-to-CX store wire (spec/03-approved/platform/store.md §6.4)']]
      [tr [td 'gRPC — HTTP/2 + protobuf'] [td 'cx-store+grpc:// (TLS: cx-store+grpcs://)'] [td 'the integration edge for gRPC-speaking systems — opt-in second listener ([grpc enabled=true addr=…]) with normative profile parity and per-call XSP-AUTH (spec/03-approved/misc/cxstore-grpc.md)']]]
    [body
      """Only the scheme on `[$store:open]` changes — a client cannot
         tell the transports apart above the URL. Three runnable,
         self-contained recipes live in the repository under
         `examples/cxstore/`, each with a Makefile that starts the
         server, drives a client, and stops it (all three were run
         green against this branch-head binary):"""]
    [list
      [item """`client-server/` — a client over THE store wire: `cx
              store-serve` holds a store and serves it over the XSP
              profile, and a client drives put/get/list/query through
              `cx-store+xsp://` — remoting is a URL change, not an API
              change."""]
      [item """`grpc/` — the same surface over the integration edge: a
              daemon config exposing one store on a gRPC listener, and a
              client driving put/get/list/query/modify through
              `cx-store+grpc://`."""]
      [item """`dir-sync/` — a directory tree of `.cxd` files gains
              content addressing, dedup, history, and name-based lookup:
              `ingest.cx` walks and stores, `materialize.cx` writes the
              named tree back out, `watch.cx` keeps the store live off a
              real OS filesystem watch. Deliberately a recipe, not a
              subcommand — customization is editing CX."""]]]

  [child id=store-security n=18.6 title='Security and encryption at rest'
    [body
      """Store security is four independent fail-closed layers — host
         capabilities at the effect point ([[capabilities]]),
         encryption at rest, service-tier grants/tenancy, and XAP-layer
         authority when a store backs a XAP (see
         [[dist-entitlements]]). The layer this subsection teaches is
         encryption: open a store with an `encrypt-key-id` and every
         object is sealed at rest (AES-256-CBC-then-HMAC, per-object
         data keys wrapped by a tenant key-encryption key). Crucially
         it is invisible to the object graph — objects stay keyed by
         the **plaintext** hash, so dedup, structural sharing, and
         replication are unchanged; only the bytes on the substrate are
         ciphertext:"""]
    [example lang=cx
      """[?lib 'cx-platform/store' :as store]
[?let [= $s [$store:open-opts 'file:///tmp/sealed-store' [map encrypt-key-id='tenant-a']]]
      [= $h [$store:put-doc $s [secret-plan [step 'first']]]]
  [$store:get-doc $s $h]]"""]
    [output
      """$ CX_STORE_KEK_tenant-a=<64 hex chars> cx --allow-read --allow-write --allow-env sealed.cx
[secret-plan [step 'first']]"""]
    [body
      """Key material never lives in config files — the KEK arrives by
         environment (`CX_STORE_KEK_<id>`). The mode is fixed at store
         creation and every mismatch is a hard error in both
         directions: an encrypted store opened without its key never
         appears empty or corrupt, encryption cannot be enabled in
         place on plaintext data, and a substrate that cannot seal
         refuses rather than degrading:"""]
    [; check=none — deliberate fail-closed error-value demo: the
       refusal is a non-capability error (CXER1100), which the
       docs-example gate would count as a failure; the output below is
       pinned from a live run. ]
    [example lang=cx check=none
      """[?lib 'cx-platform/store' :as store]
[$store:open-opts 'mem://' [map encrypt-key-id='tenant-a']]"""]
    [output
      """[err code=cx-err:CXER1100 message='E_STORE_UNRESOLVED_BACKEND: encryption-at-rest (encrypt-key-id) is supported on the sealing substrates — `file://…` (pack, the default), `file://…?encoding=object-per-key`, `sqlite://…`, or `s3://…` (got scheme=mem); refusing to store plaintext for mem://']"""]
    [body
      """KEK rotation is a shipped operator verb: `cx store-rotate-kek`
         re-wraps every envelope's data key under a new tenant KEK —
         payloads and content addresses untouched, atomic per object,
         resumable, fail-closed (the same operation is callable
         in-process as `[$store:rotate-kek]`) — and on a store that was
         never sealed there is nothing to re-wrap, so the verb refuses
         rather than pretending:"""]
    [fixture id=store-rotate-kek-001-plaintext-unsupported]
    [body """The shipped rotation, end to end:"""]
    [output
      """$ CX_STORE_KEK_tenant-a=… CX_STORE_KEK_tenant-b=… cx store-rotate-kek \
    --url file:///tmp/sealed-store --encrypt-key-id tenant-a --new-key-id tenant-b
[rotation-report objects=5 rewrapped=5 already-current=0 from=tenant-a to=tenant-b]
cx store-rotate-kek: 5 object(s) now under `tenant-b` (5 re-wrapped) — verify reads, then destroy the old KEK `tenant-a`."""]
    [body
      """Service-tier grant authority and tenancy sit above this: the tenant
         boundary is **store-per-tenant**, so each tenant owns a
         separate dedup pool — a shared pool would let one tenant probe
         another's content by hash, and the design rejects that
         existence oracle by construction. Scoping rides the authority
         basis rather than a principal-to-store-name list: a grant, and
         any delegation attenuated from it, is bound to its mount, and a
         cross-tenant delegation is a fault, never something that
         quietly compiles to nothing. A class the row does not hold is
         denied as a value — `[deny …]` carrying its code as data, which
         is what lets the daemon send the same value across the wire
         verbatim:"""]
    [fixture id=store-grants-003-ungranted-class-denies-as-a-value]
    [body
      """Three least-privilege habits: open registries and read paths
         with `[map read-only='true']` so `write` is never requested;
         scope net grants to the daemon (`--allow-net=127.0.0.1:18762`),
         never bare `--allow-net`; and keep `--allow-all` for trusted
         local runs. A principal's seed lives in a `0600` file and
         reaches the process through `xsp-seed-env` — never a URL, an
         opts literal or the config text; `${env:VAR}` injection keeps
         TLS key material out of the config file; the request log
         carries no credential material. When a store backs a XAP, a
         fourth layer sits above these: grants attach to features and
         principals at the policy enforcement point, never to library
         code, install-time consent is exactly the manifest's `needs`
         set, and an entitlement check is a policy check
         ([[dist-entitlements]]). What refuses rather than degrades,
         in one table:"""]
    [table
      [tr [th 'surface'] [th 'behaviour']]
      [tr [td 'a missing capability'] [td 'CXER0271, naming the grant']]
      [tr [td 'a write on a read-only store'] [td 'CXER1110']]
      [tr [td 'an integrity mismatch on read'] [td 'CXER1120 — never a silent wrong document']]
      [tr [td 'a substrate persist failure'] [td 'CXER1116 — never a phantom success']]
      [tr [td 'an encrypted store with the wrong or absent key'] [td 'a hard error, in both mode directions']]
      [tr [td 'an unsupported compression request'] [td 'rejected, never accepted and ignored']]
      [tr [td 'an unknown daemon config attribute'] [td 'startup failure CXER1711; a reload refused whole, CXER1712']]
      [tr [td 'an anonymous or under-privileged wire op'] [td 'CXER1702 (401) / CXER1703 (403); over the store wire the PEP\'s [deny …] value verbatim']]
      [tr [td 'a package that fails verification at any stage'] [td 'nothing staged; the stage\'s own error value']]]]

  [child id=store-console n=18.7 title='The management console'
    [body
      """Fleet-facing management has a UI: the store management
         console, which lives in its **own repository**
         (`xap-store-console`) and is never embedded in the daemon. It
         is itself a XAP built from feature packages (the distribution
         system of the next section, eating its own dog food),
         connecting to daemons over the XSP store profile with a
         DID-held credential — so everything it can do is a public,
         grant-gated protocol op, and the console doubles as proof the
         management API is complete. The free tier covers connect, health, metrics,
         status, browse, config reload, and maintenance (gc). Contract:
         the store management console spec
         (`spec/03-approved/misc/store_management_console.md`), read
         through the wire-transition note at its head, which maps every
         store HTTP op verb-for-verb onto the profile. Client identity
         is a DID presented over the wire (open-opts
         xsp-did/xsp-seed-env); operator authority rides
         `[xsp [grants …]]`. The console's own principal is minted the
         way every principal is — `cx store-mint-principal`, offline,
         granting nothing until an operator installs the stanza
         ([[ops-bootstrap]]) — and gets the narrowest classes its tier
         needs: `read` covers browsing (get, list, iter, query); the
         whole admin plane — `status`, `gc`, `mounts`, `config-reload`
         — sits in `admin`."""]]
]
